<p>We no longer live in a time when college freshmen get shocked by much of the reading material assigned to them. Counterintuitively, the texts that do retain their power to disturb young minds (presuming they can <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2026/08/why-americans-stopped-reading-books.html">read texts in the first place</a>) tend to skew old — and in one prominent case, about 2,400 years old. Even the most superficial interpretations of <a href="https://amzn.to/4AtJrCa">Plato’s <em>Republic</em> </a>challenge the political assumptions held by almost every student today, at least in the developed West. Many of them hold those assumptions so deeply that they’ve never articulated them. <span style="font-size: 13pt;">Encountering the arguments made by Plato’s Socrates in favor of a society ruled by an enlightened upper class, tightly controlled from above in everything from the division of its labor to the content of its art, prompts them to do so. </span></p>
<p>Or rather, it prompts them to do so in an ideal educational setting, but then, Plato was dedicated to nothing so much as the ideal. When he casts Socrates as making the case for the best possible relationships between ruler, state, and individual, he creates less a how-to manual (as <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2022/06/machiavellis-the-prince-explained-in-an-illustrated-film.html">Machiavelli did nineteen centuries later</a>, in a brutally quasi-satirical spirit) than a means of better understanding extant societies by bringing their characteristics into relief through comparison.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>We may reflexively quote Churchill’s remark about modern democracy, vulnerable as it is to manipulation by all manner of unsavory actors, being “the worst form of Government except for all those other forms that have been tried from time to time,” but we nevertheless flinch from holding it up too closely against any kind of ideal system.</p>
<p>It’s tempting to declare that we wouldn’t want to live in the republic of the <em>Republic</em>. (Some of us may have succumbed to that temptation in the political-philosophy lecture hall.) But a more reflective reading reveals a good deal about Plato’s time as well as our own. In <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oAp_POUGgB8">the 78-minute video above</a>, Jared Henderson takes us through the Socratic dialogues that propose and refine this Utopia, with its philosopher-kings, noble lies, spiritual aristocrats, breeding festivals, and guardians trained from the cradle. He arrives at certain unfashionable ideas,&nbsp;such as the ultimate task of refining one’s own soul&nbsp;and the necessity of persuading those impervious to philosophy with myth. Even&nbsp;those of us who regard ourselves as sophisticated thinkers,&nbsp;impervious to the crude rhetoric of&nbsp;ideologies and demagogues, would do well to ask ourselves: how much of that thinking has been shaped by the stories we’ve heard all our lives?</p>
